Jutlandic exhibits many diphthongs not present in standard Danish. The long stressed mid vowels {{IPA|/eː, øː, oː/}} are diphthongised as {{IPA|/iə, yə, uə/}} respectively in central Jutland and the South Schleswig dialect, e.g. {{lang|jut|ben}} {{IPA|[ˈbiˀən]}} = Standard Danish {{IPA|[ˈbeːˀn]}} 'leg', {{lang|jut|bonde}} 'farmer' {{IPA|[ˈbuəɲ]}} = Standard Danish {{IPA|[ˈbɔnə]}} (< ''bōndi'').

South Jutlandic has the same vowel quality for these vowels, but exhibits a tonal distinction, which is present in place of the Danish {{lang|da|stød}}. Northern Jutlandic raises them without diphthongising them to {{IPA|/iː, yː, uː/}} respectively. In Hards, a small area of Mid Western Jutland, the vowels become diphthongised with a glide much like in English, and are pronounced as {{IPA|/ej, øj, ow/}}.

In Northern Jutland, {{IPA|/iː, yː, uː/}} are also diphthongised in two syllable words with a glide. Northern Jutlandic always has the glide present ({{IPA|/ij, yj, uw/}}) and North Western Jutlandic tends towards the glide, but it is not present for all speakers. Long {{angbr|a}} and {{angbr|å}} have been raised to {{IPA|[ɔː]}} and {{IPA|[oː]}} respectively in northern Jutlandic, e.g. {{lang|da|sagde}} 'said' {{IPA|[ˈsɔː]}} = Standard Danish {{IPA|[ˈsæː(ə)]}}, {{lang|da|gå}} 'go, walk' {{IPA|[ˈɡoːˀ]}} = Standard Danish {{IPA|[ˈɡ̊ɔːˀ]}}.

